# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored) and fill from `npx supabase status -o env`.
#
# No name here carries the `NEXT_PUBLIC_…` prefix, and that is the point: such a
# variable is inlined into the client bundle, and QĐ-25 says the browser never
# talks to Supabase. Reads happen in Server Components, writes in Server
# Actions, and no request leaves the app's own origin. The Supabase guides do
# prefix the first two, because they assume a browser client exists; this
# project has none.
#
# The publishable (anon) key is safe to hand to a client — row level security is
# what protects the data — but it still has no business in the bundle here.

SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=paste-the-publishable-key-from-supabase-status

# The SERVICE ROLE key. It bypasses row level security, so it is read by
# things that run outside the app — `npm run seed:users`, which creates the
# eight demo shoppers and the demo manager (`app_metadata.role = "admin"`, which
# only this key can write) through the admin API, and the database tests — and,
# since slice B3c, by exactly one server-only module of the app,
# `lib/db/service.ts`, for the product-photo bucket: storing an uploaded photo,
# removing one, emptying `up/` on "Đặt lại dữ liệu mẫu". Since slice B4 the
# daily `/api/reset` also calls `demo_anchor()` and `reset_demo()` with it,
# because a cron job has no session. Since slice B4b it also counts the rate
# limits (`take_rate()`, `tidy_rate_hits()`, callable by the service role only)
# and is the HMAC key that turns a visitor's address into the counter's
# subject, and the daily reset sets back, through the Auth admin API, any of the
# nine demo accounts DEMO_PASSWORD no longer opens. No table is read or written with it
# directly. Without it the app runs with NO rate limits (they fail open, and the
# server log says so once), a photo upload answers "không tải được ảnh", and the
# daily reset answers 503.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=paste-the-secret-key-from-supabase-status

# The password every demo account shares — the shoppers and the manager — and a
# PUBLIC one: the sign-in screen prints it beside the demo emails, because this
# is a portfolio demo and an account nobody can open is a demo nobody can look
# at. It still comes from the environment rather than from a source file,
# because a password written into a repository is a habit worth not forming.
# Unset means no "Đăng nhập thử" or "Vào quản trị thử" button and no
# credentials line: a control that cannot do what it says is not drawn.
DEMO_PASSWORD=xemthu-2026

# Slice B16, "Tiếp tục với Google": the OAuth client from Google Cloud. NOT for
# `.env.local` and not read by the app — the Supabase CLI reads them, for
# `env()` in `supabase/config.toml` ([auth.external.google]), and the local
# auth server is the only thing that holds them. Put the two lines, uncommented
# and filled in, in `supabase/.env`, then `npx supabase stop` and
# `npx supabase start`. Measured on CLI v2.117.0 (07/10/2026): the CLI reads
# both `supabase/.env` and a `.env` at the project root (the root one wins when
# both exist), but Next.js loads a root `.env` into the server's environment as
# well, where a Google secret has no business — so `supabase/.env`.
# `.gitignore`'s `.env*` keeps both files out of git. Without the two values
# the stack still starts (`npm run test:db` needs nothing from Google), and the
# button sends the visitor to Google with the literal text
# `env(SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID)` as the client id. The hosted
# project keeps its own copy in the dashboard (Authentication → Providers →
# Google), never in a file.
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=paste-the-client-id-from-google-cloud
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=paste-the-client-secret-from-google-cloud

# Slice B18, paying by card on Stripe's page, TEST MODE ONLY (QĐ-42, QĐ-46).
# Both names come from Vercel's Stripe integration (Vercel Marketplace, "Install
# New Stripe Sandbox", connected to the project), which writes them into the
# project's environment; on a developer machine STRIPE_SECRET_KEY goes in
# `.env.local`. Read by one server-only module, `lib/stripe.ts`, which refuses
# any key that is not a test key (`sk_test_…`) — card payment is then off: the
# order is still placed, and its page says no payment page could be opened.
# A sandbox nobody claims is deleted after 60 days: make a new one, then
# redeploy.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=paste-the-sandbox-secret-key-it-starts-with-sk_test
# NOT USED, and not to be referenced anywhere: Next inlines every
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_…` variable a source file names into the browser's JavaScript,
# and the browser never talks to Stripe — it only navigates to Stripe's page.
# The integration adds it to the Vercel project; it may stay there, unread.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=not-used-by-this-app

# Optional. Set only where Vercel Cron calls the two daily jobs in `vercel.json`,
# `GET /api/health` and `GET /api/reset`: Vercel sends the value as an
# `Authorization: Bearer …` header, and both routes require exactly that as soon
# as this variable exists. Left unset on a developer machine, where
# `/api/health` answers without it and the daily reset is OFF — `/api/reset`
# answers 404, because emptying the demo is never open to whoever asks. To try
# the reset locally, set it in the shell that runs `next start`, not here.
# CRON_SECRET=
